
<p>Mark Zuckerberg, on X:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Muse Spark Open — weights releasing today — runs great on new
Mac Studios thanks to work with @johnternus and Apple.</p>
</blockquote>

<p><a href="https://x.com/johnternus/status/2110789142326165518">John Ternus, replying</a>:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Congrats to the team! Amazing to run a model of Muse Spark Open’s
caliber and scale this efficiently, right from your desk. Mac
Studio keeps pushing the boundaries of on-device AI!</p>
</blockquote>

<p>You will recall that the M5 Ultra Mac Studio with 512 GB RAM is <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/08/apple-introduces-new-mac-studio-with-m5-max-and-m5-ultra/">set to ship by “the end of October”</a>, and we’re midway through the month. You will also recall, from that same announcement, that multiple Macs can be clustered together via Thunderbolt — four Mac Studios can be connected to each other.</p>

<p>For Ternus, I could do without the second exclamation mark. For Zuckerberg, it’s fascinating that he had to post this on X, despite owning Threads, because no one at Apple uses Threads.</p>

<p>Zuckerberg has been posting somewhat regularly to X for months now, particularly regarding AI. Alex Heath asked him about it on his Sources podcast a month ago. <a href="https://x.com/alexeheath/status/2097460319794889088">From that one-minute clip</a>:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Threads is doing great. I think it’s actually ... either bigger
than X at this point, or it’s like very soon about to be. But, I
mean, look, there are different communities in the different
places. There’s a lot of AI folks on X, and I think part of what
you try to do with social media is just communicate where people
are, right?</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Lastly, there’s <a href="https://x.com/alexandr_wang/status/2110780784051474666?s=46">this “1984” spoof</a> posted by Alexandr Wang, Meta’s chief of AI <a href="https://cheekypint.transistor.fm/35">and mulletry</a>, which I find both amusing and painful.</p>

